## Missing Algorithmic Detail (Audit Fix): RaptorQ Codec Integration Points (Encode/Decode API Surface)

The bead references RaptorQ but omits the cancellation checkpoint integration for codec loops from §4.12.1.

### RaptorQ Cancellation Checkpoint Integration (§4.12.1)

FrankenSQLite MUST place `cx.checkpoint()` at RaptorQ decode/encode loops (every fixed number of symbol operations). This ensures:
- Long-running encoding of snapshot data can be cancelled mid-operation
- Decoding of received symbols respects deadline budgets
- No cancellation-unaware hot loops in the codec path

Pattern:
```rust
// RaptorQ encode loop with cancellation checkpoints
async fn encode_snapshot_symbols(cx: &Cx, data: &[u8], config: &RaptorQConfig) -> Result<Vec<EncodedSymbol>> {
    let encoder = RaptorQEncoder::new(config);
    let mut symbols = Vec::new();
    for (i, block) in data.chunks(config.symbol_size).enumerate() {
        if i % config.checkpoint_interval == 0 {
            cx.checkpoint_with(format!("raptorq encode symbol {i}"))?;
        }
        symbols.push(encoder.encode_symbol(block));
    }
    Ok(symbols)
}

// RaptorQ decode loop with cancellation checkpoints
async fn decode_symbols(cx: &Cx, symbols: &[EncodedSymbol], config: &RaptorQConfig) -> Result<Vec<u8>> {
    let mut decoder = RaptorQDecoder::new(config);
    for (i, symbol) in symbols.iter().enumerate() {
        if i % config.checkpoint_interval == 0 {
            cx.checkpoint_with(format!("raptorq decode symbol {i}"))?;
        }
        decoder.add_symbol(symbol);
        if decoder.can_decode() {
            break;
        }
    }
    decoder.decode().map_err(Into::into)
}
```

### Bulkhead Isolation (§4.15)

RaptorQ encode/decode MUST run behind `bulkhead` combinator to isolate heavy work with bounded parallelism so it cannot starve the sequencer or VDBE. Combined with `governor` for global concurrency budget enforcement.

### Failure Probability Monitoring (related: bd-1hi.7)

RaptorQ failure probability is monitored via e-processes (§4.3) to detect systematic codec failures under the anytime-valid framework.

# 5B.6: Remove hydration loop from open_storage_cursor for write cursors

## What

Currently, `open_storage_cursor()` in VdbeEngine has a "hydration" step: when opening a
B-tree cursor for writing, it copies ALL rows from MemTable into the B-tree cursor's pages.
This is because the B-tree starts empty (real pages aren't populated), so existing data must
be hydrated from MemDatabase.

Once CREATE TABLE allocates real root pages (5A.3) and data lives in the pager, this
hydration is unnecessary and harmful (it's O(N) per cursor open and wastes pager pages).

## Where to change

**Primary:** `crates/fsqlite-vdbe/src/engine.rs` — `open_storage_cursor()` method

## Current code (conceptual)

```rust
fn open_storage_cursor(&mut self, cursor_id: i32, root_page: i32, writable: bool) -> bool {
    if let Some(txn_io) = &self.txn_page_io {
        let shared_io = txn_io.clone();

        // *** HYDRATION: Copy MemTable rows into B-tree pages ***
        // This creates B-tree pages from scratch using MemDatabase data
        if let Some(db) = &self.db {
            if let Some(table) = db.get_table(root_page) {
                // Write empty leaf-table page
                let empty_page = make_empty_leaf_page(PAGE_SIZE);
                shared_io.write_page(page_num, &empty_page)?;

                // Create cursor and insert all rows
                let mut cursor = BtCursor::new(shared_io.clone(), page_num, PAGE_SIZE, true);
                for row in &table.rows {
                    let record = encode_record(&row.values);
                    cursor.table_insert(&cx, row.rowid, &record)?;
                }
            }
        }

        let cursor = BtCursor::new(shared_io, page_num, PAGE_SIZE, true);
        self.storage_cursors.insert(cursor_id, StorageCursor { cursor, writable });
        true
    } else {
        false
    }
}
```

## Target code

```rust
fn open_storage_cursor(&mut self, cursor_id: i32, root_page: i32, writable: bool) -> bool {
    if let Some(txn_io) = &self.txn_page_io {
        let shared_io = txn_io.clone();
        let page_num = PageNumber::new(root_page as u32)
            .expect("root_page must be valid PageNumber");

        // NO HYDRATION — data already lives in pager from real B-tree operations
        // The root page was allocated by CREATE TABLE (5A.3) and rows inserted
        // by prior INSERT operations (5B.2). Just open the cursor.

        let cursor = BtCursor::new(shared_io, page_num, PAGE_SIZE, true /* is_table */);
        let backend = CursorBackend::Txn(cursor);
        self.storage_cursors.insert(cursor_id, StorageCursor {
            cursor: backend,
            cx: Cx::background(),
            writable,
            root_page,
        });
        true
    } else {
        false
    }
}
```

## Why this matters

The hydration loop is:
- O(N) in table size per cursor open — devastating for large tables
- Wasteful of pager pages (creates temporary B-tree that's thrown away)
- Incorrect once real pages exist (would overwrite committed data)
- A bottleneck for any write operation (every INSERT opens a cursor)

## Considerations

- This task MUST be done AFTER 5A.3 (real root page allocation) — otherwise there are
  no real pages to open the cursor on
- After this change, opening a cursor on a table that was never CREATE'd will fail
  (no root page exists). This is correct behavior.
- The MemPageStore variant of CursorBackend should remain for unit tests that don't
  want a full pager stack

## Tests

- `test_cursor_opens_without_hydration`: Open cursor, verify no extra pages written
- `test_cursor_reads_existing_btree_data`: INSERT via B-tree, close, reopen cursor, read
- `test_cursor_on_nonexistent_page_fails`: Open cursor on unallocated page → error

# UPDATE: 5B.6 is now a late-stage "safety net removal" task

## Revised dependency position

**Original deps**: 5B.6 depends on 5B.1 (only)
**Revised deps**: 5B.6 depends on 5B.4, 5A.4, AND 5D.1

5B.6 (remove hydration loop) is now positioned as a **safety net removal** task that runs
AFTER verifying the new write + commit + schema-loading paths all work correctly.

## Why the dependency on 5D.1 (COMMIT through pager)?

The hydration loop in `open_storage_cursor()` (engine.rs) copies ALL rows from MemTable
into B-tree pages when a storage cursor is opened for writing. This is needed because
historically the B-tree was empty — data lived only in MemDatabase.

After Phase 5B (write-through), new data goes to the B-tree. But what about data that was
there BEFORE Phase 5B? The B-tree has it from the .db file (loaded by pager), but only if:
1. The .db file was written correctly (5D.1 — COMMIT through pager)
2. The schema was loaded from sqlite_master (5A.4 — schema loading on open)

Only after BOTH conditions are met is it safe to remove the hydration loop, because the
B-tree is guaranteed to have all data via the pager path.

## What to remove

In `crates/fsqlite-vdbe/src/engine.rs`, the `open_storage_cursor()` function:

```rust
fn open_storage_cursor(&mut self, cursor_id: i32, root_page: u32, is_write: bool) -> Result<()> {
    // ... create StorageCursor ...

    // ===== THIS IS THE HYDRATION LOOP — REMOVE IT =====
    if is_write {
        if let Some(db) = &self.db {
            if let Some(table) = db.get_table_by_root_page(root_page) {
                for row in table.iter() {
                    storage_cursor.insert(row.rowid, &row.encode())?;
                }
            }
        }
    }
    // ===== END HYDRATION LOOP =====

    self.storage_cursors.insert(cursor_id, storage_cursor);
    Ok(())
}
```

## Verification before removal

Before removing the hydration loop:
1. Verify INSERT → COMMIT → close → reopen → SELECT returns same data (5B.2 + 5D.1 + 5A.4)
2. Verify CREATE TABLE → INSERT → COMMIT → close → reopen → INSERT more → data consistent
3. Run regression guard (5F.1) after removal

## Updated tests (with logging)

```
[5B6][test=no_hydration][step=insert] rows=100 via=btree_write_through
[5B6][test=no_hydration][step=commit] via=pager_commit
[5B6][test=no_hydration][step=reopen] path={path}
[5B6][test=no_hydration][step=select] rows=100 via=storage_cursor ✓
[5B6][test=no_hydration][step=verify] all data matches original ✓
```

- `test_no_hydration_data_survives_reopen`: INSERT, COMMIT, reopen → data via pager (no hydration)
- `test_no_hydration_write_cursor_starts_empty`: open_storage_cursor for write → cursor positioned at start, no pre-populated data
- `test_no_hydration_regression_guard`: Run full test suite after removal → 0 regressions

# PARITY-B2: Fix codegen_insert_select to use IPK value as rowid

## Context

Same bug pattern as PARITY-B1, but in the INSERT...SELECT code path. When `INSERT INTO t SELECT ...`
copies rows from a source query, the IPK column from the source must be used as the target rowid.

This is exercised by the e2e concurrent-write tests that use `INSERT INTO t2 SELECT * FROM t`.

## What to Change

**File**: `crates/fsqlite-vdbe/src/codegen.rs`
**Function**: `codegen_insert_select()` (lines 2086-2231)

### Current Code (Broken)

```rust
// Lines 2184-2192: ALWAYS auto-generates rowid
b.emit_op(Opcode::NewRowid, write_cursor, rowid_reg, concurrent_flag, P4::None, 0);
```

The source query's projected columns are read into `val_regs` via `emit_column_reads()`,
but the IPK column among those values is never extracted to use as the rowid.

### Required Fix

Same pattern as B1, applied after `emit_column_reads()`:

```rust
// After reading projected columns into val_regs:
emit_column_reads(b, read_cursor, columns, src_table, src_table_alias, schema, val_regs)?;

// Route rowid based on IPK
if let Some(ipk_idx) = ctx.rowid_alias_col_idx {
    let ipk_reg = val_regs + ipk_idx as i32;
    let auto_label = b.alloc_label();
    let done_label = b.alloc_label();

    b.emit_op(Opcode::IsNull, ipk_reg, auto_label, 0, P4::None, 0);
    b.emit_op(Opcode::MustBeInt, ipk_reg, 0, 0, P4::None, 0);
    b.emit_op(Opcode::Copy, ipk_reg, rowid_reg, 0, P4::None, 0);
    b.emit_op(Opcode::Goto, 0, done_label, 0, P4::None, 0);

    b.resolve_label(auto_label);
    b.emit_op(Opcode::NewRowid, write_cursor, rowid_reg, concurrent_flag, P4::None, 0);
    b.emit_op(Opcode::Copy, rowid_reg, ipk_reg, 0, P4::None, 0);

    b.resolve_label(done_label);
} else {
    b.emit_op(Opcode::NewRowid, write_cursor, rowid_reg, concurrent_flag, P4::None, 0);
}
```

## Important Difference from B1

In INSERT VALUES, the values come from literal expressions. In INSERT SELECT, they come from
`emit_column_reads()` which reads from the source cursor. The source may be a different table
with its own IPK — the projected columns from the source are already evaluated into registers
by the time we need to check the target table's IPK.

**Critical**: The `ipk_idx` from `ctx.rowid_alias_col_idx` refers to the TARGET table's schema
position. If the INSERT SELECT has an explicit column list that reorders columns, this index
must be remapped relative to the column mapping. This is the same edge case as B4 (column list
handling) and should be addressed there.

## Acceptance Criteria

- `INSERT INTO t2 SELECT * FROM t` where both tables have IPK preserves rowid values
- `probe_insert_returning_multi_row` test continues to pass
- No regressions in existing INSERT SELECT tests

## Dependency

This task should be done AFTER bd-13b8 (B1) since the pattern is identical and B1 establishes
the reference implementation.

# Task 5.2: FrankenDBProvider Context

## What
React Context provider that manages the FrankenSQLite Worker lifecycle, initialization, and provides the database instance to child components.

## Implementation
```tsx
interface FrankenDBContextValue {
    db: FrankenDB | null;
    loading: boolean;
    error: Error | null;
    persistence: PersistenceType;
}

const FrankenDBContext = createContext<FrankenDBContextValue>({
    db: null, loading: true, error: null, persistence: 'auto'
});

export function FrankenDBProvider({
    dbName,
    persistence = 'auto',
    onReady,
    onError,
    children,
}: FrankenDBProviderProps) {
    const [state, setState] = useState<FrankenDBContextValue>({
        db: null, loading: true, error: null, persistence
    });

    useEffect(() => {
        // Skip initialization on server
        if (typeof window === 'undefined') return;

        let cancelled = false;
        FrankenDB.open(dbName, { persistence })
            .then(db => {
                if (!cancelled) {
                    setState({ db, loading: false, error: null, persistence });
                    onReady?.(db);
                }
            })
            .catch(error => {
                if (!cancelled) {
                    setState({ db: null, loading: false, error, persistence });
                    onError?.(error);
                }
            });

        return () => {
            cancelled = true;
            state.db?.close();
        };
    }, [dbName, persistence]);

    return (
        <FrankenDBContext.Provider value={state}>
            {children}
        </FrankenDBContext.Provider>
    );
}

export function useDB(): FrankenDB {
    const { db, loading, error } = useContext(FrankenDBContext);
    if (error) throw error;
    if (loading || !db) throw new Error('FrankenDB not initialized. Wrap with <FrankenDBProvider>');
    return db;
}
```

## SSR Safety
- `typeof window === 'undefined'` check prevents Worker creation on server
- Context provides loading state so components can render loading UI during hydration
- No WASM loading during SSR — React hydrates with loading state

## Acceptance Criteria
- Provider initializes database in useEffect (client-only)
- Clean error handling with onError callback
- Cleanup on unmount (close Worker)
- SSR-safe (no errors during server rendering)
- Multiple providers for different databases supported

