# 5I.5: Wire UPDATE to Maintain Index Entries

## Objective

Make UPDATE statements automatically maintain index entries when indexed columns change.

## SQLite Behavior to Match

When executing:
```sql
UPDATE t SET name = 'bob' WHERE id = 1;
```

SQLite internally does:
1. Find the row(s) to update
2. For each row:
   a. Read OLD column values
   b. Compute NEW column values
   c. For each index whose columns are affected:
      - Delete old index entry (using old values)
      - Insert new index entry (using new values)
   d. Update the table row

## Optimization: Skip Unchanged Indexes

If an UPDATE doesn't touch any columns in an index, that index doesn't need maintenance:
```sql
CREATE INDEX idx_name ON t(name);
UPDATE t SET age = 31 WHERE id = 1;  -- idx_name is NOT touched
```

The codegen should check which indexes are affected and only maintain those.

## VDBE Opcode Sequence

```
OpenWrite    P1=0  P2=table_root    # Table cursor
OpenWrite    P1=1  P2=idx_name_root # Index cursor (if name is being updated)
...
# For each row found by WHERE clause:
# 1. Read old values for index keys
Column       P1=0  P2=1  P3=old_name  # Read old 'name' value
# 2. Delete old index entry
MakeRecord   P1=old_name P2=2 P3=old_key  # old value + rowid
IdxDelete    P1=1  P2=old_key
# 3. Compute and apply new values (existing UPDATE logic)
...
# 4. Insert new index entry
MakeRecord   P1=new_name P2=2 P3=new_key  # new value + rowid
IdxInsert    P1=1  P2=new_key
```

## Implementation Changes

### 1. codegen/update.rs - Generate Index Maintenance Opcodes

```rust
fn codegen_update(
    ctx: &mut CodegenContext,
    table: &TableSchema,
    indexes: &[IndexSchema],
    set_clauses: &[(usize, Expr)],  // (column_idx, new_value)
    where_clause: Option<&Expr>,
) -> Result<(), CodegenError> {
    // Determine which indexes are affected
    let affected_indexes: Vec<_> = indexes.iter()
        .filter(|idx| {
            idx.column_indices.iter()
                .any(|col_idx| set_clauses.iter().any(|(c, _)| c == col_idx))
        })
        .collect();

    // For each affected index, generate delete-old, insert-new
    for index in &affected_indexes {
        // ... generate opcodes
    }
}
```

### 2. Handle Rowid Updates

If the rowid/INTEGER PRIMARY KEY is being updated:
- ALL indexes must be maintained (rowid is part of every index key)
- The table row itself moves (rowid is the key)

This is a special case that's more complex.

### 3. Handle NULL in Indexes

SQLite allows NULL values in indexes (unless UNIQUE NOT NULL):
- NULL values are indexed
- Two NULLs are considered different for UNIQUE purposes
- Comparison: NULL < any non-NULL value

## Test Cases

```rust
#[test]
fn test_update_maintains_index() {
    let conn = Connection::open(":memory:").unwrap();
    conn.execute("CREATE TABLE t (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, name TEXT)").unwrap();
    conn.execute("CREATE INDEX idx_name ON t(name)").unwrap();
    conn.execute("INSERT INTO t VALUES (1, 'alice')").unwrap();

    // Update name
    conn.execute("UPDATE t SET name = 'bob' WHERE id = 1").unwrap();

    // Old name not findable
    let rows = conn.query("SELECT id FROM t WHERE name = 'alice'").unwrap();
    assert_eq!(rows.len(), 0);

    // New name findable
    let rows = conn.query("SELECT id FROM t WHERE name = 'bob'").unwrap();
    assert_eq!(rows.len(), 1);
}

#[test]
fn test_update_non_indexed_column_skips_index() {
    let conn = Connection::open(":memory:").unwrap();
    conn.execute("CREATE TABLE t (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, name TEXT, age INT)").unwrap();
    conn.execute("CREATE INDEX idx_name ON t(name)").unwrap();
    conn.execute("INSERT INTO t VALUES (1, 'alice', 30)").unwrap();

    // Update non-indexed column
    conn.execute("UPDATE t SET age = 31 WHERE id = 1").unwrap();

    // Index still works (wasn't modified)
    let rows = conn.query("SELECT age FROM t WHERE name = 'alice'").unwrap();
    assert_eq!(rows.len(), 1);
    assert_eq!(rows[0].get(0), SqliteValue::Integer(31));
}

#[test]
fn test_update_rowid_maintains_all_indexes() {
    let conn = Connection::open(":memory:").unwrap();
    conn.execute("CREATE TABLE t (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, name TEXT)").unwrap();
    conn.execute("CREATE INDEX idx_name ON t(name)").unwrap();
    conn.execute("INSERT INTO t VALUES (1, 'alice')").unwrap();

    // Update rowid (moves the row)
    conn.execute("UPDATE t SET id = 2 WHERE id = 1").unwrap();

    // Index still points to correct row
    let rows = conn.query("SELECT id FROM t WHERE name = 'alice'").unwrap();
    assert_eq!(rows.len(), 1);
    assert_eq!(rows[0].get(0), SqliteValue::Integer(2));
}
```

## Dependencies

- Depends on bd-so1h (5I.3) INSERT index - uses same IdxInsert
- Depends on bd-34se (5I.4) DELETE index - uses same IdxDelete
- This is the most complex of the three DML operations

